Q: Pass and return the 3rd argument from argv A: <p>To expand on how the <code>*</code> in front of <code>argv</code> is not part of its name: you wrote code that happens to compile, by accident, but does not mean what you intend.</p>
<p>The declaration <code>char *argv[]</code> is an array of <code>char*</code>.  Technically, because it is a function parameter, it decays to a pointer to <code>char*</code>, but that doesn't happen to matter here. C allows you to use pointer operations on arrays and vice versa (except that assignment and <code>sizeof</code>, neither of which you use,  mean different things).</p>
<p>Therefore,<code>*argv</code> means the same thing as <code>argv[0]</code>, that is, the first <code>char*</code> in the array. Here, that is the filename used to invoke the executable. This matches the type you gave the parameter of <code>filename</code>. You then dereference that <code>char*</code> function argument with an array subscript, which is allowed (if and only if the <code>char*</code> actually points to a buffer with at least four elements, which you do not check) and gets you a <code>char</code>.</p>
<p>At this point, the compiler seems to have choked on the type error. The program therefore silences this error by putting <code>&amp;</code> in front of the dereference <code>char</code>. This happens to get you a pointer to a substring of the program name that skips the first three characters. This compiles, but does nothing useful.</p>
<p>So a function that extracts this one string from <code>argv</code> might look like:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>// The position of the input file path in the argument list.
#define FILENANE_POS 3U

/* The only reason C still allows you to reference command-
 * line arguments with char* instead of const char* is
 * backward-compatibility with code written before
 * const existed.  Always use const char* for immutable
 * strings in new code.
 */
static const char* filenameFromCmdLine(
    const int argc,
    const char* argv[])
{
    if (argc &lt;= FILENAME_POS) {
         // FIXME: handle the error!
    }
    return argv[FILENAME_POS];
}
</code></pre>

Q: Caused by: android.database.sqlite.SQLiteException: no such table: BOOK (code 1 SQLITE_ERROR) A: <p>What I'm doing in my app is exactly the same thing, creating a &quot;my_db.sql&quot; -&gt; saving it into the &quot;raw&quot; folder and then copying it and creating my DB at runtime.</p>
<p>I had the same issue. The app seemed to work fine on all lower versions of Android except the Pie. After much deliberation and analysis, what worked for me is adding one single line &quot;db.disableWriteAheadLogging();&quot;  in the &quot;onOpen()&quot; method.</p>
<pre><code>@Override
public void onOpen(SQLiteDatabase db) {
    super.onOpen(db);
    if (Build.VERSION.SDK_INT &gt;= Build.VERSION_CODES.JELLY_BEAN) {
        db.disableWriteAheadLogging();
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: How to make when image 2 OR image 1 is in hover change ONLY image 1 content A: <p>What you need is the <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Guides/Selectors/Selectors_and_combinators#next-sibling_combinator" rel="nofollow noreferrer">relative selector</a> (<code>a + b</code> or <code>a ~ b</code>):</p>
<pre><code>.computer:hover + .text, .text:hover
</code></pre>
<p>Which means</p>
<ul>
<li>when <code>.computer</code> is hovered, target its next sibling element <code>.text</code></li>
</ul>
<p>OR</p>
<ul>
<li>when <code>.text</code> is hovered</li>
</ul>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.computer {
  position: absolute;
  width: 350px;
  height: 419px;
  left: 1535px;
  top: 660px;
}

.text {
  position: absolute;
  left: 1570px;
  top: 455px;
}

.computer:hover + .text, .text:hover {
  width: 318px;
  height: 185px;
  content: url("https://file.garden/aG3rdTNO_Dlar7-g/new%20site/landing/home%20text.png");
  transition-delay: 3s;
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;div class="computer"&gt;
  &lt;img src= "https://file.garden/aG3rdTNO_Dlar7-g/new%20site/landing/computer.png"/&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;


&lt;div class="text"&gt;
  &lt;img src="https://file.garden/aG3rdTNO_Dlar7-g/new%20site/landing/welcome%20text%20box.png" width="318" height="185" id="text"&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<hr />
<blockquote>
<p>Quick note: this works because <code>.text</code> is preceded by <code>.computer</code> structurally. It won't work if the order is swapped. If that's the case, you would need <code>:has(&gt; .computer:hover) &gt; .text</code> to make it work without them being sensitive to their order.</p>
</blockquote>
